  "final_text": "**No, not now. Rework CRAFT first.** Both of the VP's arguments fall apart once you check the raw data.\n\n## The VP's two arguments, checked\n\n**1. \"Units up 22.4% on August.\"** That's a monthly total, and the months aren't the same length. September has 5 weekly buckets and August has 4. I counted the `week_start` values in `demand_actuals.csv`, and the report says the same in §6.\n- Monthly total: 7,488 → 9,162 units, **+22.4%**\n- Per week: 1,872 → 1,832 units, **−2.1%**\n\nSo CRAFT demand per week went down slightly. The growth comes from the extra week.\n\n**2. \"Defects fell from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000 units.\"** That rate counts NCR *records* (24 → 23, roughly flat) and divides by a unit count that grew only because of the extra week. If you count defective *quantity* instead (`qty_affected`), nothing improved:\n\n| CRAFT | Aug (4 wks) | Sep (5 wks) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| NCR records | 24 | 23 |\n| NCRs per 1,000 units (report's metric) | 3.21 | 2.51 |\n| Units affected | 2,324 | 2,832 |\n| **Units affected ÷ units sold** | **31.0%** | **30.9%** |\n| Scrapped units | 924 | 1,539 |\n| Scrap per week | 231 | 308 (+33%) |\n| Weld-crack NCRs | 2 | 5 |\n\nFor comparison in September: NEST 10.6%, PULSE 8.5%, AURA 5.8%, and ECHO, BREW and BEAM have no NCRs. CRAFT's affected volume is about 3× the next-worst line, and the share of it being scrapped went from 40% to 54%.\n\n**Limit of this metric:** `qty_affected` is production quantity and `units_actual` is sales, so the 31% is not a defect rate on customer units. The report also says NCRs aren't returns (§6). It's a consistent signal, but not an exact rate.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n- **Decision:** Hold the extra Q3 marketing for CRAFT. Put the effort into rework first, starting with weld cracks, dimensional issues and documentation, which caused 18 of September's 23 NCRs.\n- **Why:** Defective quantity is about 31% of units sold, the same in both months. Scrap is rising. More demand would mean more production on a process that hasn't improved.\n- **Risk if we wait:** We might give up some premium revenue. But per-week demand isn't growing (−2.1%), and margin is unknown because the data has no cost figures (§6), so the €450 average price doesn't show that CRAFT is profitable.\n- **Risk if we spend:** More scrap, more rework cost, and possibly field failures on a €199–€699 product. The weld cracks are the worst of these.\n- **Counter-position:** The VP's metric (NCR records per 1,000 units) does show 3.21 → 2.51. That holds only if you count records, not units, and accept the 4-week vs 5-week comparison.\n- **What would change the call:** Revisit when CRAFT's units affected stay below about 10% of units sold (near NEST's level) for two months in a row, scrap per week falls, and per-week demand is flat or up on a same-number-of-weeks basis. I set the trigger in quantities on purpose. Counting records could \"improve\" just by merging NCRs into fewer, larger ones.\n\n## Where the numbers come from\n\nI recalculated CRAFT's September revenue from the raw files: **€4,124,238** = `demand_actuals.csv` (95 CRAFT rows with a September `week_start`, 9,162 units) × `base_price_eur` from `product_lines.csv`. That matches the report exactly. The NCR figures come from `ncr_records.csv`, filtered to `line = CRAFT`, by month of `ncr_date`, summing `qty_affected`. Those also match the report's 23 NCRs and 2,832 units.\n\nTwo things we can't tell from this data:\n- Whether CRAFT is profitable, because there are no cost figures.\n- What marketing would actually achieve, because the €2.03M budget is set per campaign and can't be traced to a line or month (§4).\n\nThere were no team metric definitions on file, so the definitions above are mine. Worth agreeing on one defect-rate definition with Quality so this argument doesn't come back next month.",
